#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# equivalent

#% text
# `equivalent(a, b, ...)` is true when its arguments are all true or all false. With `true` among them it is the conjunction of the others, with `false` the conjunction of their negations, with both it is `false`; repeated arguments are dropped, and a call with two or more distinct symbolic arguments stays as it is.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
equivalent(a, b)
#% output
#= equivalent(a, b)

#% cell
equivalent(a, b, a, b, c)
#% output
#= equivalent(a, b, c)

#% cell
equivalent(a, b, true)
#% output
#= a and b

#% cell
equivalent(a, true, false)
#% output
#= false

#% cell
boolean_table(equivalent(a, b), [a, b])
#% output
#= [true, false, false, true]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `xor`, `implies`, `is_tautology`
